  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA dry but cool weekend is in store for central Kentucky and northern \nIndiana as surface high pressure builds in over the region. WIth \nmostly clear skies and calmer north-northwesterly winds, \ntemperatures will bottom out in the low to mid 30s by Saturday \nmorning. A Frost Advisory remains in effect until 9 AM EST, with any \nfrost that does develop melting in the morning hours shortly after \nsunrise. There is the potential for patchy fog to develop over the \nnext few hours, primarily in river valleys across the western \nportions of the area. After the dissipation of any fog that does \ndevelop, it will be a mostly clear Saturday across the region, save \nfor some upper-level clouds that may form in the afternoon. With \nnnorth-northwestely flow prevailing, afternoon highs on Saturday \nwill be 5-10 degrees below normal for this time of year, with \ntemperatures in the low to mid 50s forecast. \n\nFor Saturday night, clearing skies and calm winds will allow for \ndecent radiational cooling to take place. The latest NBM has a 55-\n70% chance for temperatures below freezing for rural areas, \ndecreasing to 40-55% for urban locations. An area-wide Freeze \nWarning was recently issued and will be in effect from 12 AM EST \nSunday - 10 AM EST. Overnight temperatures are currently forecast to \nbe in the upper 20s/low 30s, with the coldest conditions expected \nfor sheltered rural areas. To close out the weekend, winds will \nshift to the southwest during the first half of Sunday before a weak \ncold front moves through and switches the surface flow back to the \nnorthwest. Temperatures will be slightly warmer than Saturday, with \nhighs in the mid to upper 50s expected.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, the stretch of dry weather will continue \nas the area of surface high pressure remains overhead. Temperatures \nwill fluctuate some Monday through Wednesday, with highs in the 60s \nforecast. Overnight lows will be be slightly warmer as well, \nhovering around the upper 30s/low 40s range. The upper-level ridge \nover the central and western CONUS that is responsible for the \nnorthwesterly flow we are currently experiencing will begin to \nbreak down some as the week progresses. By midweek, long range \ndeterministic and ensemble models are showing a brief period of \nzonal flow over the Ohio Valley before another shift in the upper-\nlevel pattern takes shape. The latter portions of next week depict \nan amplified upper-level pattern with ridging over the western CONUS \nand troughing over the east. For us, guidance is having a shortwave \ntrough swing over the region, though the exact timing is still \nuncertain. This change in the pattern will allow for the return of \nrain chances Wednesday night-Friday, however antecedent dry air \ncurrently depicted in guidance would keep our rainfall totals down, \nwith the most recent LREF only gives a 15-25% chance for more than \n0.25\" of rain. The end of next week could bring a return to cooler \ntemperatures with highs in the upper 40s/50s as guidance is \ndepicting a colder air mass filling behind the system as it swings \nthrough.",
